{
  "id": "dukotah-fixed-numeric-keys",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Rotation, hash and cuneiform keys",
  "question": "Do the three pinned Campaign VII numeric-key groups produce English-scoring complete LP2 outputs under their exact additive GP29 transforms?",
  "reader_question": "Could the rotation sequence, LP2-56 hash or cuneiform numbers serve as keys?",
  "summary": "Three archived searches over 12 named numeric streams and 5,376 complete settings reproduce; none clears their −5.2 English-score bar. Planted controls show 285 of 288 exact constructed messages clear that bar, so the result is scoped to the named keys and source rules.",
  "finding": "B1 Mayan rotations: best −6.895 on LP2-22, 0/896 above bar. B4 printed-hash streams: best −6.679 on LP2-49, 0/2,240. C2 cuneiform/digit strings: best −6.725 on LP2-51, 0/2,240. All three original scripts reproduce archived JSON; independent scalar checks match all 5,376 complete outputs and 55 source/site pages.",
  "limitations": "The −5.2 bar misses three of 144 correct F-null planted messages, and literal-F plants sometimes replace an original known rune with F to satisfy the source rule. This does not exclude other numerical keys, phases, clocks, transcripts or scorer models. The 2012 and cuneiform provenance descriptions are source attributions, not separately verified historical claims.",
  "next_steps": "State an exact numerical sequence, page alignment and F-clock rule before another search. Test complete known text under that same rule and reserve an independent manuscript segment for confirmation.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
  "tags": [
    "LP2",
    "numeric key",
    "running key",
    "Mayan rotations",
    "hash expansion",
    "cuneiform",
    "source replay",
    "positive control"
  ],
